C GBP D1 7,2,6.5 y x
R GB GBP
H GBK W8 8.5,2,6.5
P 8.35,2,6.5 blue
P 8.65,2,6.5 blue
R RJIK GBK
R GB GBDP
R RW6OK GBDK
! COUNTERWEIGHTS. A rigid linkage backdrives: the arm's own weight turns the levers, and if the
! operator lets go the arm falls. There is no hydraulic lock and no useful friction here, so each
! lever carries a counterweight that cancels its share of the arm at the drawn pose. Two rules the
! hard way: hang the weight BELOW the lever shaft or the balance is an inverted pendulum and will
! not stand up, and take the sign from the four-bar rather than from intuition - three of these five
! sit on the opposite side of the lever from where I first put them. Masses are solved, not guessed:
! measure each lever's drift in the first 60 ms from the drawn pose, build the 5x5 sensitivity matrix
! and solve it. cw5 and cw6 hang on their own crank pins, which is the same thing with fewer parts.
C CW2 W8 -6,0,2.5 y x+z
R LV2 CW2
W cw2 CW2 mass=172
C CW3 W8 -1,-0.7,5.5 y -x+z
R LV3 CW3
W cw3 CW3 mass=245
C CW4 W8 -1,0.7,5.5 y -x+z
R LV4 CW4
W cw4 CW4 mass=340
W cw5 LV5C mass=100
W cw6 LV6C mass=100
